  "reason": "No experiments have been completed, so there is zero evidence and maximal uncertainty across all design dimensions (modality, encoder, profile, aggregation, fusion). The most informative first experiment is the simplest possible baseline: rna_only, singleprofile, PCA (2000 dims). This establishes the performance floor for the RNA modality with a linear, interpretable encoder and no added architectural complexity. Every other configuration in the search space adds one or more layers of complexity (learned encoders, multiprofile aggregation, image modality, fusion) on top of this baseline, so knowing its accuracy and F1 is essential to calibrate whether those added components actually contribute. It also trains quickly and reliably, reducing the risk of a wasted experiment due to training instability. Under a limited budget, anchoring the search with this minimal reference point maximizes the interpretive value of all subsequent experiments."
}

